Every Cloud Engineer has been there: you open the AWS Billing Dashboard and see a spike. The culprits? Usually "orphaned" resources—Elastic IPs that aren't attached to any instance, or EBS volumes from terminated instances that were never deleted.In this post, I’ll show you how to automate the detection of these resources using Python and Boto3.The Problem with Manual Cleanup If you only use one region, you might find these manually. But if you operate in multiple regions or manage multiple accounts, it becomes a nightmare. AWS charges you for Elastic IPs even if you aren't using them! Here is a basic snippet to find unattached Elastic IPs in your default region:import boto3
def find_unattached_ips(): client = boto3.client('ec2') addresses = client.describe_addresses() for addr in addresses['Addresses']: if 'InstanceId' not in addr: print(f"Found unattached IP: {addr['PublicIp']}")
if __name__ == "__main__": find_unattached_ips() Taking it to the Next Level The script above is a good start, but for production environments, you need:Multi-Region Scanning: To check all 20+ AWS regions automatically.Multi-Account Support: To switch between different CLI profiles.Dry Run Mode: To simulate deletions before they happen.🚀 Save Time with the Professional Suite I've spent the last few days building a robust version of this tool that handles all the heavy lifting (Global scan, Multi-account, and Safety checks).If you want to skip the coding part and start saving money on your AWS bill today, you can get my AWS Cloud Cost Killer script here:Includes the Python source code, instructions, and lifetime updates.
